# Working on this API

This module is an HTTP API built on [mkunion](https://github.com/widmogrod/mkunion)
unions. You define shapes (queries, commands, answers) as Go types and write
handler bodies; `go generate` derives the handler interfaces, JSON, dispatch
and clients. Nothing generated has a default: a handler is complete or it
does not compile.

## The loop

    go generate ./... && go test ./... -race

Run it after every change to a `//go:tag mkunion` type block. `go generate`
prints "generated: ..." lines; that is normal. Never edit `*_gen.go` files:
they are rewritten every run.

## Layout

    api/server.go                  composition root: hand-written list of mounts, auth, GET /v1 catalogue
    api/server_test.go             clients over HTTP and in-process; TestEveryResourceIsMounted
    api/resource/<r>/query.go      <R>Query union: one variant per question           (edit)
    api/resource/<r>/command.go    <R>Command union: one variant per order            (edit)
    api/resource/<r>/answer.go     <R>Answer union: answer types + Error; codes; Refuse (edit)
    api/resource/<r>/handler.go    Handler: one HandleX method per variant            (edit)
    api/resource/<r>/store.go      in-memory store; swap for a real one               (edit)
    api/resource/<r>/handler_test.go                                                  (edit)
    api/resource/<r>/endpoint.go   glue: QueryEndpoint/CommandEndpoint, error mapping (leave)
    api/resource/<r>/*_gen.go      generated                                          (never)
    cmd/server/main.go             flags, builds Deps, ListenAndServe

Routes: `POST /v1/<r>/query`, `POST /v1/<r>/command`, `GET /v1` (catalogue).
A request on the wire is `{"$type":"<r>.GetX","<r>.GetX":{...}}`; the answer
is one `<R>Answer` variant in the same form. HTTP 200 for every answer,
`Error` included; 400 when the body is not a request; 401 for a bad token.

## Add a query (or a command)

1. In `query.go` (`command.go` for a command) add a variant to the tagged
   `type (...)` block:

       ListTasks struct{ f.Returns[*TaskList] }

   `f.Returns[*A]` names the answer. `A` must be a variant of the resource's
   Answer union in `answer.go`. If it is new, add it there in the same step:

       TaskList struct {
           Items []Task `json:"items"`
       }

   Field names on the wire come from `json` tags; always write them. Lists
   live inside a struct, never bare.
2. `go generate ./...`
3. `go test ./...` now fails with
   `*Handler does not implement TaskQueryHandler (missing method HandleListTasks)`.
   Add the method to `handler.go`, signature exactly as the generated
   `TaskQueryHandler` interface in `query_union_gen.go` says:

       func (h *Handler) HandleListTasks(ctx context.Context, op *ListTasks) (*TaskList, error)

4. Add a test in `handler_test.go`. Run the loop.

## Refuse a request

A handler says no by returning a refusal in place of the answer:

    return nil, Refuse(ErrNotFound, "task %q is unknown", op.ID)

The client receives `Error{Code, Message}` as the answer (HTTP 200). Codes are
constants in `answer.go`; add one per new kind of refusal. Any other Go error
a handler returns reaches the client as `Error{Code: ErrInternal}`.

In a test, `errors.As(err, &refused)` with `var refused *Error` works both
on the handler method and through a client.

## Add a field

Add it to the struct with a `json` tag and run the loop. Clients ignore
unknown fields, so adding is safe; renaming or removing breaks old clients.

## Add a resource

    go tool mkunion init resource --name project

writes `api/resource/project/` with `GetProject`, `CreateProject`, `Project`,
`Error` and tests, and prints what to paste. Then:

1. `api/server.go`: add `Project *project.Handler` to `Deps` and the two
   mount lines to `New`. Until you do, `TestEveryResourceIsMounted` fails
   with `resource "project" has no route`.
2. `cmd/server/main.go`: build the handler in `Deps`.
3. Run the loop.

A resource with no commands: delete `command.go`, `CommandEndpoint` in
`endpoint.go`, the `_ ...CommandHandler` line and the `HandleX` methods in
`handler.go`, the tests in `handler_test.go` that call them, and the
`.../command` mount line. Then run

    go tool mkunion clean ./api/resource/project

before the loop: generated files are never removed for you, and the stale
`command_*_gen.go` and `types_reg_gen.go` would fail with
`undefined: CreateProject` / `undefined: ProjectCommand`. Same for a
resource with no queries, and whenever a file with a `//go:tag` block is
deleted or renamed.

## Messages and what they mean

| you see | it means |
|---|---|
| `missing method HandleX` | add `HandleX` to `handler.go` |
| `undefined: TaskQueryHandler`, `undefined: DispatchTaskQuery` | `go generate` has not run, or failed: run it and read its output |
| `variant X answers with *Y, which is not a variant of TaskAnswer` | `Y` must be declared in the `TaskAnswer` block in `answer.go` |
| `variant X has no f.Returns` | every query and command embeds `f.Returns[*A]` |
| `answers=TaskAnswer names no union in package task` | the Answer union tag in `answer.go` was renamed or removed |
| `resource "x" has no route` (test) | mount it in `api/server.go` |
| `wrong type for method HandleX` | the signature must match the generated interface exactly; copy it from `*_union_gen.go` |

## Test

Handler methods are plain functions: call them. To see what a client sees
without HTTP, build the generated client on the endpoint's `Send`:

    client := NewTaskQueryClient(QueryEndpoint(h).Send)
    task, err := rpc.Call(ctx, client, &GetTask{ID: id})   // task is *Task

`api/server_test.go` runs the same assertions over HTTP and in-process.

## Run

    go run ./cmd/server -addr 127.0.0.1:8080 -token secret
    curl -s localhost:8080/v1 -H 'Authorization: Bearer secret'
    curl -s -X POST localhost:8080/v1/task/command -H 'Authorization: Bearer secret' \
      -d '{"$type":"task.CreateTask","task.CreateTask":{"title":"first"}}'

## Export the contract

    go tool mkunion shape-export --language swift -o <dir> api/resource/*/query.go api/resource/*/command.go api/resource/*/answer.go
